{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787598913,"nonce":"SOHglXZBJAICCvieGujEvXr2uhc=","message":"Recommend CLOSE — both the cited wiring and the underlying waste died on the\n0067 branch.\n\nThe premise was: workspace_cas in main.rs is the raw S3Storage, so\ndrive_workspace's drain ingests straight into cold, the service never sees a\nroot, /have (warm-only) reports everything missing, and the warm hit rate lies.\n\nAt HEAD of feat/adr-0067-pack-is-the-record:\n- The control-plane drain leg no longer exists at all: drains are in-Pod into\n  the Depot's packs (ADR-0067, one-pass durable; flush_to_cold is deleted).\n  The Depot sees every byte by construction — it is the sole durable writer.\n- main.rs:263 wires workspace_cas exactly as this ticket asked: when the Depot\n  is configured, it is TieredCas(warm = WorkspaceClient.cache_only_cas(),\n  cold = object store) with fall_through_on_warm_error; the None arm is\n  dev-only and the executor fail-closes on it.\n- /have no longer answers from warm at all — it answers from the durable pack\n  index in Postgres (durable_present_of over depot_pack_members), so drain\n  dedup works across replicas and restarts, which is stronger than what this\n  ticket requested.\n- \"warm hit rate measures the wrong thing\" is resolved by ADR-0066/0067's\n  reframe: warm is explicitly a cache; a first read on a fresh replica ranging\n  into a pack and backfilling warm is the designed behaviour, not a bypass.","files":null}]}